# Isolated M32/M64 QKV traversal experiment

This draft changes only the Q/K/V traversal inside the C256 vector-cache
variant3 kernel. It includes neither the new B-prefetch experiment nor a public
dispatch change. Nothing here has been compiled or measured yet.

The current cached implementation visits shared FFN input independently for Q,
K and V. The original native loop instead reuses each shared A fragment for all
three matrices. Its SASS has four shared128-bit loads and48 QMMAs per K32 step,
while the separate cached traversals load A three times. This is the concrete
reason to test fusion; it is not evidence that fusion will improve latency.

Two variants are provided. M32 completes Q/K/V for two M16 tiles at a time;
M64 completes all four tiles together. For each K32 step the draft loads the
six Q/K/V B vectors once, reads each participating shared A fragment once, and
uses that A fragment for12 independent N8 MMAs. Every accumulator receives the
same eight ascending K32 updates, the same operands and the same zero seed as
before. Only the traversal of independent accumulators changes.

At the end of each M group, Q uses the unchanged `normalize_c(...,head_scale)`
and `c_to_a`; K uses `normalize_c(...,1.f)` and `c_to_a`; V uses the original
two `pack_pair` calls and `transpose_v`. Each helper still executes in all32
lanes. Q/K normalization and V transposition operate on a single M16 tile, so
changing when independent tiles finish does not change a reduction scope.
Attention, projection, publication and every one of the seven barriers are
unchanged. The shared FFN epoch remains live until the original B6 boundary.

The K loop stays rolled. The one/two M groups are unrolled to keep result array
indices static. This bounds future-K fragment lifetimes without intentionally
prefetching a later B group. The existing vector-cache operand preparation,
alignment copies and validation are reused. Preparation is outside resident
timing, as it is for the retained cache/B-prefetch alternatives.

## Costs the experiment must measure

The following are source-level counts per complete256-thread C256 CTA. They
are logical operand traffic and lane events, not NCU counters or measured cache
traffic. Compiled SASS must confirm that B is reused and A is not duplicated.

| QKV implementation | B read bytes | Shared A read bytes | Raw words per thread | Earlier published words at last M group |
|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|
| Existing separate traversal |196608|393216|32|up to32|
| Fused M32 |393216|131072|48|24|
| Fused M64 |196608|131072|96|0 before publication|

Both drafts preserve98304 per-lane MMA events across the CTA and publish3072
Q/K/V M16 lane fragments exactly once. M32 trades an extra196608 global weight
bytes for a smaller raw accumulator set. M64 preserves B traffic but has96 raw
words plus24 current B words,4 current A words, the retained FFN skip and other
kernel state. These counts are not register-allocation predictions. Spills,
instruction scheduling or occupancy could erase the shared-load savings.

No M16 variant is included: its fourfold QKV B reload is a poor first control
when the requested M32 and M64 choices already expose the main tradeoff.

## Files and contracts

- `csrc/kernel_impl/window_block_qkv_fused.cuh.draft`
- `csrc/kernel_launcher/window_block_qkv_fused.cu.draft`
- `csrc/torch_api/window_block_qkv_fused.cpp.draft`
- `tools/draft_window_qkv_fused.py.draft`
- `tools/window_qkv_fused_proof.py.draft`
- `tests/test_window_qkv_fused.py.draft`
- `tests/benchmark_window_qkv_fused.py.draft`

The private call is `_inference_window_block_qkv_fused(...,phase,
packed_output=True,raw_output=False,tile_rows=32)`, with tile_rows32 or64.
It accepts the same five prepared dual caches as the existing C256 cache
operator, Half or packed E4 input, packed output and optional raw Half output.
The base schedule is forced to rolled K plus coalesced output stores, matching
old vector-cache variant3. Unsupported row choices and Half output are rejected.
No training API or model hook changes are included.

The generator pins the active vector-cache source and writes only `.draft`
files. Reversing the inserted helper, its call site and the new template
parameter reproduces every executable source token outside the QKV experiment.
The CPU proof separately enumerates every head/lane/M/K/column operand and
compares each accumulator's full history to the old traversal. It validates
cache bounds/alignment, matrix coordinates, shared-address bounds, B reuse,
and exactly-once publication. Results are in
`outputs/window_qkv_fused_proof.json.draft`.

Independent CPU review found no C++/API arithmetic, bounds or barrier blocker.
It confirmed M32's two disjoint M groups, M64's single group, unchanged
per-accumulator operands/K order and uniform full-warp publication helpers.
The reviewer caught a test-only byte-view issue on noncontiguous Half inputs;
the draft comparisons now call `contiguous()` before viewing bytes.

The116 planned GPU cases cover both tile sizes, all phases, ragged/batched
shapes, packed/Half input, raw/no-raw output, all four canonical and all four
cache variants, the separate B-prefetch and earlier pair-prefetch alternatives,
independent composition, numerical patterns, real blocks15/49, misaligned and
noncontiguous operands, mutable captures and gradient rejection. They have only
been syntax-checked and counted on CPU.

The benchmark compares13 implementations with identical retained output
contracts: canonical0–3, cache0–3, pair-prefetch, B-prefetch, current public,
QKV-M32 and QKV-M64. It uses deterministic shuffled order with an optional
reversed order, checks an independent composed oracle, verifies immutable
operands and separately checks three poisoned graph replays for each new
candidate. The report keeps each new candidate's ratio against the best old
competitor and cache3. It does not require a3% gap between M32 and M64.

After independent review, compile all supported architectures, inspect each
SM120 register/spill report and the QKV loop's actual shared/global loads, then
run exactness, sanitizer and fair resident timing gates. A local win would
still require a new original-kernel physical-I/O comparison before any native
speed claim. The existing C256 cache's physical-I/O comparison was slower than
the original despite its local resident improvement; that limitation remains.
